  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Monday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Tuesday and Wednesday will still have \nchances for additional showers and thunderstorms, but at a lower \nchance (10-30%) and more across eastern Nevada as the high \npressure system retrogrades west towards Arizona. Thursday \nthrough Sunday, models are showing better agreement for the \nweather pattern forming, with the upper level ridge maintaining a\nposition across the desert southwest. An uptick in the convective\nactivity is possible for Friday into the weekend as a piece of \nenergy from the eastern Pacific crosses into California and into \nnorthern and central Nevada and will need to be watched. For \nMonday, the models start to become different with each other but \nnot by much, with both showing a trough forming over the northwest\nand cresting into Nevada, however ECMWF is showing the trough to \nbe weak and short lived, while GFS is showing the trough to be \nstronger with the chance for more moisture. Either outcome will \nstill have much cooler temperatures for the day with a \nsouthwesterly flow present, yet is uncertain in regards to \nmoisture. Temperatures will remain near or above normal for the\nweek with highs in the 90s the first half of the week and \ndropping into the 80s over the weekend. Winds will be breezy in \nthe afternoons with speeds up to 10-15 mph, gusts 20-25 mph. These\nwinds with drier air will cause some elevated fire weather \nconcerns for Humboldt County, leaving it drier than others.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Near and short term forecast\nmodifications were required to account for residual moisture and \ninstability across the forecast area. Weather elements modified \ninclude PoP, Wx, wind, wind gust and sky grids.",
